IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc3-recognizance.l4 -- Criminal Code (Canada), PART II.1 Terrorism, "Recognizance with Conditions": sections 83.3 to 83.33.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively), written by part agent ccc3 from the text alone. The text is a plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws Website
-- consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the official text. Section 83.28 and 83.29 (repealed) are in `ccc3-proceedings`.
--
-- WHO THIS IS ABOUT. This is the part of Part II.1 that acts on a person who has done nothing yet: a peace officer lays an information, the person may be arrested without a warrant
-- and detained, taken before a provincial court judge, released or held, and made to enter into a recognizance with conditions. The functions answer what the peace officer, the judge
-- and the person may, must or must not do, so that the person in the seat can see what is done to them and by when.
--
-- READING A RESULT (rule of 2026-10-29 and the ruling of 2026-11-12): as in `ccc3-interpretation.l4`. A power ("may") with an "if" gate answers NOTHING when the gate fails (tag GATE-A);
-- a duty ("shall") whose "if" or "unless" is unmet or met answers JUST FALSE (the duty does not arise or is lifted; fork F-ccc3-6, F-ccc3-5); a flat limit ("may not exceed", "not more than")
-- answers JUST FALSE. The clock of the regulative rules is HOURS from the arrest. A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Judgments the text hands to the officer or the judge ("believes on reasonable grounds", "suspects on reasonable grounds", "is satisfied", "without unreasonable delay") are plain inputs.

§ `Section 83.3 -- Recognizance with conditions`

§§ `83.3(1) to (3) the information`

-- (1) "The Attorney General’s consent is required before a peace officer may lay an information under subsection (2)": a bar, JUST TRUE until consent; once consent is given it says nothing more.
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-16 | s 83.3(1) the peace officer is barred from laying an information | s 83.3(1)
-- src:1795 | Recognizance with Conditions
-- src:1796 | Attorney General’s consent
-- src:1797 | 83.3 (1) The Attorney General’s consent is required before a peace officer may lay an information under subsection (2).
@ref s 83.3(1) (src:1795-1797)
GIVEN `the Attorney General has consented to the peace officer laying the information` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(1) the peace officer is barred from laying an information` MEANS
    IF `the Attorney General has consented to the peace officer laying the information`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "consent is required before a peace officer may lay an information"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (2) GATE-A: "Subject to subsection (1), a peace officer may lay an information before a provincial court judge if the peace officer (a) believes ...; and (b) suspects ...".
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-20 | s 83.3(2) the peace officer may lay an information before a provincial court judge | s 83.3(2)*
-- src:1798 | Terrorist activity
-- src:1799 | (2) Subject to subsection (1), a peace officer may lay an information before a provincial court judge if the peace officer
-- src:1800 | (a) believes on reasonable grounds that a terrorist activity may be carried out; and
-- src:1801 | (b) suspects on reasonable grounds that the imposition of a recognizance with conditions on a person, or the arrest of a person, is necessary to prevent the carrying out of the terrorist activity.
@ref s 83.3(2) (src:1798-1801)
GIVEN `the Attorney General has consented to the peace officer laying the information` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that a terrorist activity may be carried out` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the imposition of a recognizance with conditions on a person, or the arrest of a person, is necessary to prevent the carrying out of the terrorist activity` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(2) the peace officer may lay an information before a provincial court judge` MEANS
    IF NOT `the Attorney General has consented to the peace officer laying the information`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Attorney General’s consent is required before a peace officer may lay an information under subsection (2)"
    ELSE IF `the peace officer believes on reasonable grounds that a terrorist activity may be carried out` AND `the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the imposition of a recognizance with conditions on a person, or the arrest of a person, is necessary to prevent the carrying out of the terrorist activity`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a peace officer may lay an information before a provincial court judge if the peace officer"

-- COVERS: encoded | s 83.3(3) the judge may cause the person to appear before a provincial court judge | s 83.3(3)
-- src:1802 | Appearance
-- src:1803 | (3) The judge who receives the information may cause the person to appear before any provincial court judge.
@ref s 83.3(3) (src:1802-1803)
GIVEN `the judge received an information under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(3) the judge may cause the person to appear before a provincial court judge` MEANS
    IF `the judge received an information under subsection (2)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The judge who receives the information may cause the person to appear"

§§ `83.3(4) to (6) arrest without a warrant, the duty of the peace officer, taking the person before a judge`

-- (4) GATE-A: "Despite subsections (2) and (3), a peace officer may arrest a person without a warrant and cause the person to be detained in custody ... if (a) either (i) the grounds ... exist but, by reason of exigent circumstances,
-- it would be impracticable to lay an information under subsection (2), or (ii) an information has been laid under subsection (2) and a summons has been issued; and (b) the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the detention ... is necessary to prevent a terrorist activity."
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-20 | s 83.3(4) the peace officer may arrest the person without a warrant and cause the person to be detained in custody | s 83.3(4)*
-- src:1804 | Arrest without warrant
-- src:1805 | (4) Despite subsections (2) and (3), a peace officer may arrest a person without a warrant and cause the person to be detained in custody, in order to bring them before a provincial court judge in accordance with subsection (6), if
-- src:1806 | (a) either
-- src:1807 | (i) the grounds for laying an information referred to in paragraphs (2)(a) and (b) exist but, by reason of exigent circumstances, it would be impracticable to lay an information under subsection (2), or
-- src:1808 | (ii) an information has been laid under subsection (2) and a summons has been issued; and
-- src:1809 | (b) the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the detention of the person in custody is necessary to prevent a terrorist activity.
@ref s 83.3(4) (src:1804-1809)
GIVEN `the grounds for laying an information referred to in paragraphs (2)(a) and (b) exist` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to lay an information under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an information has been laid under subsection (2) and a summons has been issued` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the detention of the person in custody is necessary to prevent a terrorist activity` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(4) the peace officer may arrest the person without a warrant and cause the person to be detained in custody` MEANS
    IF     (    (`the grounds for laying an information referred to in paragraphs (2)(a) and (b) exist` AND `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to lay an information under subsection (2)`)
             OR `an information has been laid under subsection (2) and a summons has been issued`)
       AND `the peace officer suspects on reasonable grounds that the detention of the person in custody is necessary to prevent a terrorist activity`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a peace officer may arrest a person without a warrant and cause the person to be detained in custody, in order to bring them before a provincial court judge in accordance with subsection (6), if"

-- (5) "If a peace officer arrests a person without a warrant in the circumstance described in subparagraph (4)(a)(i), the peace officer shall, within the time prescribed by paragraph (6)(a) or (b), (a) lay an information in accordance with subsection (2); or (b) release the person."
-- The duty as a decision (does it arise) and as a regulative rule (MUST lay an information OR release, WITHIN 24 hours when a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours, otherwise with no number of hours, the text saying "as soon as feasible").
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-6 | s 83.3(5) the peace officer must lay an information or release the person; s 83.3(5) the duty to lay an information or release the person | s 83.3(5)*
-- src:1810 | Duty of peace officer
-- src:1811 | (5) If a peace officer arrests a person without a warrant in the circumstance described in subparagraph (4)(a)(i), the peace officer shall, within the time prescribed by paragraph (6)(a) or (b),
-- src:1812 | (a) lay an information in accordance with subsection (2); or
-- src:1813 | (b) release the person.
@ref s 83.3(5) (src:1810-1813)
GIVEN `the peace officer arrested the person without a warrant in the circumstance described in subparagraph (4)(a)(i)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(5) the peace officer must lay an information or release the person` MEANS
    IF `the peace officer arrested the person without a warrant in the circumstance described in subparagraph (4)(a)(i)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If a peace officer arrests a person without a warrant in the circumstance described in subparagraph (4)(a)(i)"

@ref s 83.3(5) (src:1810-1813)
DECLARE `s 83.3(5) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 83.3(5)(a) lays an information in accordance with subsection (2)`
    `s 83.3(5)(b) releases the person`

@ref s 83.3(5) (src:1810-1813)
GIVEN `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 83.3(5) act`
`s 83.3(5) the duty to lay an information or release the person` MEANS
    IF `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested`
    THEN    (PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(5)(a) lays an information in accordance with subsection (2)` WITHIN 24 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "neither an information was laid nor the person released within 24 hours")
         ROR (PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(5)(b) releases the person` WITHIN 24 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "neither an information was laid nor the person released within 24 hours")
    ELSE    (PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(5)(a) lays an information in accordance with subsection (2)` HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "neither an information was laid nor the person released as soon as feasible")
         ROR (PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(5)(b) releases the person` HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "neither an information was laid nor the person released as soon as feasible")

-- (6) "Unless a peace officer is satisfied that a person should be released from custody without conditions ... and so releases the person, the person detained in custody shall be taken before a provincial court judge ...:
-- (a) if a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested, ... without unreasonable delay and in any event within that period; and (b) if ... not available within 24 hours ..., as soon as feasible."
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-6 | s 83.3(6) the rule for taking the person before a provincial court judge; s 83.3(6)(a) the person is taken before a provincial court judge in time; s 83.3(6) the duty to take the person before a provincial court judge | s 83.3(6)*
-- src:1814 | When person to be taken before judge
-- src:1815 | (6) Unless a peace officer is satisfied that a person should be released from custody without conditions before their appearance before a provincial court judge in accordance with the rules in paragraph (a) or (b), and so releases the person, the person detained in custody shall be taken before a provincial court judge in accordance with the following rules:
-- src:1816 | (a) if a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested, the person shall be taken before a provincial court judge without unreasonable delay and in any event within that period; and
-- src:1817 | (b) if a provincial court judge is not available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested, the person shall be taken before a provincial court judge as soon as feasible.
@ref s 83.3(6) (src:1814-1817)
DECLARE `s 83.3(6) rule` IS ONE OF
    `s 83.3(6)(a) the person is to be taken before a provincial court judge without unreasonable delay and in any event within 24 hours`
    `s 83.3(6)(b) the person is to be taken before a provincial court judge as soon as feasible`
    `s 83.3(6) the person is not to be taken before a provincial court judge because the peace officer released the person without conditions`

@ref s 83.3(6) (src:1814-1817)
GIVEN `the peace officer is satisfied that the person should be released from custody without conditions before their appearance, and so releases the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 83.3(6) rule`
`s 83.3(6) the rule for taking the person before a provincial court judge` MEANS
    IF `the peace officer is satisfied that the person should be released from custody without conditions before their appearance, and so releases the person`
    THEN `s 83.3(6) the person is not to be taken before a provincial court judge because the peace officer released the person without conditions`
    ELSE IF `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested`
         THEN `s 83.3(6)(a) the person is to be taken before a provincial court judge without unreasonable delay and in any event within 24 hours`
         ELSE `s 83.3(6)(b) the person is to be taken before a provincial court judge as soon as feasible`

@ref s 83.3(6) (src:1814-1817)
GIVEN `the number of hours after the arrest at which the person is taken before a provincial court judge` IS A NUMBER
      `it was without unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(6)(a) the person is taken before a provincial court judge in time` MEANS
    IF `the number of hours after the arrest at which the person is taken before a provincial court judge` AT MOST 24 AND `it was without unreasonable delay`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "without unreasonable delay and in any event within that period"

@ref s 83.3(6) (src:1814-1817)
DECLARE `s 83.3(6) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 83.3(6) takes the person before a provincial court judge`

@ref s 83.3(6) (src:1814-1817)
GIVEN `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 83.3(6) act`
`s 83.3(6) the duty to take the person before a provincial court judge` MEANS
    IF `a provincial court judge is available within 24 hours after the person has been arrested`
    THEN PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(6) takes the person before a provincial court judge` WITHIN 24 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "the person was not taken before a provincial court judge within 24 hours after the arrest"
    ELSE PARTY `a peace officer` MUST `s 83.3(6) takes the person before a provincial court judge` HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY `a peace officer` BECAUSE "the person was not taken before a provincial court judge as soon as feasible"

§§ `83.3(7) to (7.2) release, or detention on cause shown, and adjournments of up to 48 hours`

-- (7) "When a person is taken before a provincial court judge under subsection (6), (a) if an information has not been laid under subsection (2), the judge shall order that the person be released; or (b) if an information has been laid ..."
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-6 | s 83.3(7)(a) the judge must order that the person be released | s 83.3(7); s 83.3(7)(a)
-- src:1818 | How person dealt with
-- src:1819 | (7) When a person is taken before a provincial court judge under subsection (6),
-- src:1820 | (a) if an information has not been laid under subsection (2), the judge shall order that the person be released; or
@ref s 83.3(7)(a) (src:1818-1820)
GIVEN `an information has not been laid under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(7)(a) the judge must order that the person be released` MEANS
    IF `an information has not been laid under subsection (2)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if an information has not been laid under subsection (2), the judge shall order that the person be released"

-- (b)(i) "the judge shall order that the person be released unless the peace officer who laid the information shows cause why the person’s detention in custody is justified on one or more of the following grounds (A), (B), (C)".
-- The grounds are a closed list ("one or more of the following grounds"). Once cause is shown the duty to release is lifted (an "unless" once met: fork F-ccc3-5).
-- COVERS: fork F-ccc3-5 | s 83.3(7)(b)(i) the judge must order that the person be released; s 83.3(7)(b)(i) the detention is justified on the ground | s 83.3(7)(b)*
-- src:1821 | (b) if an information has been laid under subsection (2),
-- src:1822 | (i) the judge shall order that the person be released unless the peace officer who laid the information shows cause why the person’s detention in custody is justified on one or more of the following grounds:
-- src:1823 | (A) the detention is necessary to ensure the person’s appearance before a provincial court judge in order to be dealt with in accordance with subsection (8),
-- src:1824 | (B) the detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public, including any witness, having regard to all the circumstances including
-- src:1825 | (I) the likelihood that, if the person is released from custody, a terrorist activity will be carried out, and
-- src:1826 | (II) any substantial likelihood that the person will, if released from custody, interfere with the administration of justice, and
-- src:1827 | (C) the detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice, having regard to all the circumstances, including the apparent strength of the peace officer’s grounds under subsection (2), and the gravity of any terrorist activity that may be carried out, and
@ref s 83.3(7)(b) (src:1821-1827)
DECLARE `s 83.3(7)(b)(i) ground` IS ONE OF
    `s 83.3(7)(b)(i)(A) the detention is necessary to ensure the person’s appearance before a provincial court judge in order to be dealt with in accordance with subsection (8)`
    `s 83.3(7)(b)(i)(B) the detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public, including any witness, having regard to all the circumstances including the likelihood that a terrorist activity will be carried out and any substantial likelihood that the person will interfere with the administration of justice`
    `s 83.3(7)(b)(i)(C) the detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice, having regard to all the circumstances, including the apparent strength of the peace officer’s grounds under subsection (2), and the gravity of any terrorist activity that may be carried out`
    `s 83.3(7)(b)(i) no ground of (A), (B) or (C)`

@ref s 83.3(7)(b) (src:1821-1827)
GIVEN ground IS A `s 83.3(7)(b)(i) ground`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(7)(b)(i) the detention is justified on the ground` MEANS
    CONSIDER ground
    WHEN `s 83.3(7)(b)(i) no ground of (A), (B) or (C)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "justified on one or more of the following grounds"
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 83.3(7)(b) (src:1821-1827)
GIVEN `an information has been laid under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer who laid the information shows cause why the person’s detention in custody is justified on one or more of the grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83.3(7)(b)(i) the judge must order that the person be released` MEANS
    IF NOT `an information has been laid under subsection (2)`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if an information has been laid under subsection (2)"
    ELSE IF `the peace officer who laid the information shows cause why the person’s detention in custody is justified on one or more of the grounds`
         THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the peace officer who laid the information shows cause why the person’s detention in custody is justified"
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (b)(ii) "the judge may adjourn the matter for a hearing under subsection (8) but, if the person is not released under subparagraph (i), the adjournment may not exceed 48 hours."
